## Item text
Free newsletter

Rucking notes by email

New gear reviews and practical training notes. Join now to get the newsletter from its first issue. Unsubscribe anytime.

Tingling under a loaded pack can come from shoulder-strap compression or traction, but numb hands do not prove rucksack palsy.

Stop, remove the pack, and end the loaded session. Persistent or recurrent symptoms and any weakness need assessment before another ruck.

A hip belt, wider padded straps, lower load, and better adjustment may reduce nerve pressure; none makes nerve injury impossible.

Seek prompt assessment for persistent numbness or weakness. Sudden one-sided numbness or weakness, facial droop, or speech trouble needs emergency help, even if it clears.

What's happening

Rucksack palsy is a load-carriage-related nerve injury involving compression or traction of the brachial plexus, with shoulder straps as the usual mechanism. Symptoms can include tingling, numbness, pain, cramping, or weakness in the upper limb (Knapik et al., 2016). Similar symptoms can also come from the neck, a single peripheral nerve, circulation, or another condition, so numb hands under a pack are not a diagnosis by themselves.

Mild tingling that settles completely after the pack comes off may be transient pressure. That doesn't establish the cause or make continuing safe. Diagnosed rucksack palsy can take months to recover, and some patients have lasting symptoms (Nylund et al., 2011). Stop, remove the pack, and end the loaded session; don't wait for weakness or persistent numbness to wear off under load.

That evidence supports a simple boundary: treat any new tingling as a signal to remove the pressure, not as proof that no damage has occurred. Do not continue rucking through weakness or persistent numbness while waiting for it to "loosen up."

The brachial plexus runs from the neck through the shoulder into the arm. A load-carriage review names heavier load, longer carrying distance, and poor load distribution as hypothetical risk factors, with strap compression or traction as the mechanism. It frames its prevention measures as ways symptoms may be reduced rather than as tested programs (Knapik et al., 2016).

01Knapik and colleagues describe rucksack palsy as a compression or traction injury and recommend reducing load, improving distribution, adjusting and padding straps, and considering a hip belt.

02Nylund and colleagues found that 30 of 38 military patients (79%) reported no symptoms at long-term follow-up; eight (21%) still reported symptoms. Symptom reports and measured muscle strength were separate outcomes.

03Dorhout Mees and colleagues reviewed 63 Dutch military cases, including 52 with sensory symptoms (83%). Their recovery table lists four complete, 44 partial and one absent recovery: 49 recorded outcomes, with a median follow-up of nine weeks. Those short, incomplete records cannot establish a permanent-disability rate.

04The exact rules in this guide, such as one finger of strap slack or a timed fit check, are practical field cues rather than clinically validated thresholds.

Five fit factors worth checking

The following factors can increase shoulder pressure or nerve traction. They are useful fit checks, not a way to diagnose the source of numbness.

High shoulder-strap pressure. Over-tightened straps can concentrate pressure across the shoulder. Straps should control the pack without creating a hard pressure line, but there is no universal "correct" notch for every body and pack.

Narrow or poorly padded straps. Narrow straps can concentrate a given load over less surface area. A load-carriage review recommends wider, better-padded straps as one way to reduce traction and compression symptoms (Knapik et al., 2016).

More load than you or the pack can distribute comfortably. In the Finnish series, heavier recalled loads at symptom onset were associated with reduced muscle strength at follow-up; they did not significantly predict self-reported symptoms (Nylund et al., 2011). That association doesn't establish a safe weight. New symptoms mean the pack comes off.

No effective load transfer. A properly fitted hip belt can shift some load away from the shoulders. The Knapik review recommends considering one, but neither a 15-pound threshold nor a guaranteed percentage of transfer applies to every pack and torso.

Holding the straps for long periods. Gripping the straps can keep the arms and shoulders in one position and can mask a poor fit. Let the arms swing naturally when terrain allows. This is a practical comfort cue; the cited studies do not establish strap-gripping as a measured cause of rucksack palsy.

What to do if your hands go numb

If tingling or numbness begins under the pack, stop rather than trying to diagnose it while walking. The first goal is to remove the pressure and see whether the symptoms settle completely.

Step 1: Take the pack off. Set it down safely and note whether the tingling, numbness, pain, or weakness changes. Removing the load is more informative than making several strap changes while the nerve is still under pressure.

Step 2: Let your arms rest in a neutral position. Gentle arm movement is fine if comfortable, but do not aggressively stretch or pull on a numb or weak arm.

Step 3: End the loaded session, even if the tingling clears. Seek prompt medical assessment for numbness that doesn't settle completely or for any weakness. Sudden one-sided numbness or weakness needs emergency help, even if it clears; don't assume the pack explains it. The warning signs below take priority over fit adjustments.

Step 4: Review fit before a later session only after symptoms settle completely. Any weakness, persistent numbness or repeated episodes need assessment before you return. Reduce the load, repack it symmetrically, and check for twisted straps, hard pressure points and an unstable load. These are proposed risk-reduction measures in the load-carriage review, not a treatment for diagnosed nerve injury.

Step 5: Refit the suspension without chasing a magic percentage. Wider padded straps and a well-fitted hip belt may reduce shoulder pressure. One finger of strap slack is a useful field cue, not a medical threshold.

There is no cited five-minute cutoff that separates harmless pressure from nerve injury. If numbness does not settle promptly and completely, or if you notice weakness, end the ruck and get medical guidance rather than testing the pack again.

Reducing shoulder pressure on future rucks

Gear can reduce specific sources of friction or poor load distribution, but no product handles a known percentage of brachial-plexus risk. Start with lower load, good fit, padded straps, and an even pack; treat the products below as equipment options, not medical devices.

Fit and load choices can reduce risk, but they cannot guarantee prevention.

Use straps that spread pressure comfortably on your body. The 2016 load-carriage review recommends wider, better-padded, properly adjusted straps. The GORUCK GR1 is one option with substantial padding, but its fit still depends on torso, load, adjustment, and whether you add a compatible hip belt. Our best rucking backpacks guide compares those fit variables.

Use a hip belt when it fits the pack and your torso well. The same review lists a hip belt among the measures that may reduce strap traction and compression, but there is no universal 20-pound cutoff and no zero-risk setup. If a pack cannot transfer load comfortably, reduce the load before buying around the problem. Body Glide Original can help with skin friction; it does not address nerve compression.

If your current bag can't hold the weight securely or accommodate a hip belt that fits you, consider those features when replacing it. The GORUCK Rucker 5.0 holds a plate high in an elevated pocket and accepts an optional hip belt. Its full review reports sore traps after a loaded hike without the belt, which is the shoulder-only carry this section is trying to avoid. That observation doesn't show that the belt prevents nerve injury; persistent or recurrent symptoms need assessment before another loaded session.

Let your arms move when terrain allows. If you need to hold the straps to keep the pack stable, revisit the packing and suspension. This is a practical fit cue rather than a clinically tested prevention rule.

Check fit during long rucks. A 15-to-20-minute check can help you notice pressure before it becomes distracting, but the interval is an editorial field cue, not a studied threshold. Stop immediately for tingling, numbness, or weakness.

Build upper-back and shoulder capacity. The review proposes resistance training as a risk-reduction measure; it doesn't establish that a particular exercise routine prevents palsy. Rows, face pulls and other tolerable upper-back work are general conditioning options once symptoms have been assessed.

Check the whole fit. Pack height, strap path, load symmetry, torso length, and hip-belt contact work together. Ask someone to check the back view under the actual training load; avoid any setup that creates a hard pressure point or requires constant strap gripping.

When to be concerned

Some under-pack tingling settles when the pressure is removed, but documented rucksack palsy is a real nerve injury. The following signs are reasons to stop and seek assessment rather than continue adjusting the pack.

Call emergency services for sudden one-sided numbness or weakness, facial droop, or speech trouble, even if it clears. Sudden weakness confined to a hand can also be a stroke warning (NICE). Arm discomfort with chest discomfort, shortness of breath, nausea or a cold sweat can signal a heart attack; seek emergency help rather than adjusting the pack (American Heart Association).

Numbness that persists after the pack comes off. There is no universal minute cutoff for "normal." Sensory change that does not settle promptly and completely, or that returns without the pack, needs assessment.

Weakness in the hand, arm, or shoulder. Reported cases can involve more than grip strength, including shoulder movement and individual nerves. Remove the load and seek prompt medical assessment for any new weakness, even if it improves. Sudden weakness follows the emergency advice above.

Numbness that occurs without rucking. Pack-related nerve symptoms can persist after a ruck. The neck, an individual nerve or another condition may also be responsible. Symptoms away from the pack deserve medical evaluation rather than another fit experiment.

Symptoms in one arm only. Backpack-related nerve injury can be one-sided; bilateral symptoms are not required. Unilateral numbness also has other possible causes, so it deserves assessment rather than a diagnosis from pack symmetry.

Any of the above symptoms. If you are uncertain, see a clinician. The correct nerve and cause are not always obvious from symptoms alone, and published case series used neurological examination and sometimes electrodiagnostic testing.

Management and next steps

If brief tingling settles completely after removing the pack and there was no weakness, review fit and reduce the load before a later session. Repeated episodes need assessment before returning. If a clinician has diagnosed rucksack palsy, follow that plan rather than treating a gear change as the whole intervention.

Recovery is variable. In the Finnish follow-up, 30/38 patients reported no symptoms after a median 4.5 years. Among those with total recovery, the median recovery time was 3.2 months, with a range of 0-9 months. Table 1 records symptoms in 8/38 (21%) and reduced strength in 5/32 examined patients (16%). The abstract's 15% prolonged-symptom figure doesn't match that symptom table. These are different outcomes in a small military series, not a personal recovery forecast.

The practical measures proposed in the load-carriage review are reducing load, improving symmetry, adjusting and padding straps, and considering a hip belt that fits correctly. The available sources do not establish a prevention percentage for a commercial pack or fit technique.

Most incidence and recovery data come from military populations carrying demanding loads, and they don't translate into a recreational incidence rate. The lesson that does carry over is to act early on symptoms and not assume a lighter civilian load makes nerve injury impossible.

Evidence boundary: the strongest sources here are military observational studies, case series, and a load-carriage review. They support the mechanism and the fit/load recommendations, but they do not validate a recreational incidence rate, a safe percentage of bodyweight, or a guaranteed prevention effect from any product.

If your hands start tingling, remove the pack and end the loaded session. Before a later, lighter ruck, symptoms should have settled completely with no weakness. Persistent or recurrent symptoms and any weakness need assessment first; no strap adjustment or timed fit check clears you to return.

Frequently Asked Questions

Some nerve injuries leave lasting symptoms, although these studies don't establish a permanent-damage rate for recreational ruckers. In the Finnish series, 30/38 patients reported no symptoms at long-term follow-up and 8/38 still had symptoms. Remove the load for new symptoms; any weakness or persistent numbness needs prompt assessment, with emergency help for sudden one-sided symptoms.

Total weight is only one factor. Strap path, padding, a hard pressure point, load position, torso fit, the neck, and a peripheral nerve can all matter. Do not assume a product upgrade will eliminate symptoms regardless of load; persistent or repeat numbness deserves assessment.

Call emergency services for sudden one-sided numbness or weakness, especially with facial droop or speech trouble, even if it clears. Backpack-related injury can also be one-sided, but don't assume that's the explanation. Remove the pack, end the session, and seek prompt assessment for persistent or recurrent symptoms.

The straps should control the load without creating a hard pressure line or numbness. One finger of space is a useful fit cue, not a universal medical rule. Load, padding, torso shape, pack height, and hip-belt fit all affect pressure.

No. Remove the pack and seek prompt medical assessment for new weakness in the hand, arm, or shoulder, even if it improves. Sudden weakness can be a stroke warning, including weakness confined to the hand, and needs emergency help. Don't keep rucking to see whether it improves.

End that session and review the fit and load before trying again. A symptom that settles after pressure is removed is reassuring, but it does not prove that continuing the same ruck is safe. Recurrent numbness or any weakness needs assessment.

Resistance training is one proposed risk-reduction measure in the load-carriage review, but no exercise guarantees prevention. Load reduction, even packing, appropriate padding, strap adjustment, and a well-fitted hip belt address the external pressure more directly.

Research transparency

Sources and evidence notes

These are the published sources checked for this article. Most rucking injury research comes from military load carriage or from studies of the same condition in other sports. When the article turns that evidence into an exact rucking step, it labels the step as an editorial starting point rather than a tested clinical protocol.

Evidence last checked: October 9, 2026

01Backpack palsy and other brachial plexus neuropathies in the military populationDorhout Mees SM, van Faals NL, van Alfen N · Journal of the Peripheral Nervous System · 2020

02Load Carriage-Related Paresthesias: Part 1: Rucksack Palsy and Digitalgia ParestheticaKnapik JJ, Reynolds KL, Orr R, Pope R · Journal of Special Operations Medicine · 2016

03Recovery of brachial plexus lesions resulting from heavy backpack use: a follow-up case seriesNylund T, Mattila VM, Salmi T, Pihlajamäki HK, Mäkelä JP · BMC Musculoskeletal Disorders · 2011

