Frequently Asked Questions

Yes. Any sturdy backpack with two padded shoulder straps works for starting. Add DIY weight such as books, water bottles or sand in a bag, wrapped or packed so it can't shift, and keep it high and close to your back.

Start with DIY options like bricks or sandbag fillers before investing in ruck plates. Sandbag fillers are inexpensive and let you adjust weight in small steps; a plate is a convenience upgrade once you know you'll keep rucking.

Often, yes, especially with lighter loads. Many people ruck comfortably in running shoes, particularly trail runners. The ones that tend to cause trouble under a heavier pack are very soft, unstable models that let the foot roll, and a firmer, more stable shoe usually feels better as the load goes up. There's no research-backed ideal heel drop, so choose by fit and comfort.

A training ruck is built to hold a small, dense weight high and close to your back, in heavy fabric that tolerates a hard plate, and it's usually carried on the shoulders. A hiking pack is designed to spread bulkier, lighter gear over a frame and hip belt; dense weight dropped into its main compartment tends to sag low unless you pack it carefully.

Not automatically. If your synthetic athletic socks fit well and stay comfortable, keep them. Merino blends are an option when your current pair stays damp, bunches or rubs, but changing the material alone doesn't guarantee a better fit or fewer blisters.

Heavy Cordura packs typically last years of regular use, and GORUCK backs its packs with a lifetime guarantee. Shoes wear out much sooner; replace them when the midsole feels flat or looks creased. Coated plates need little care beyond a wipe-down and dry storage.

Yes. GORUCK's Standard plates have a 9 x 11.5 inch footprint sized for GORUCK's pockets, and its Long plates measure 9.5 x 15 inches. Other brands' plates vary in footprint and thickness, so check a plate's dimensions against your pack's pocket before buying.

